# Template for the production environment file.
#
# Copy to /opt/family-manager/.env on the VPS and fill in real values there:
#   scp infra/.env.example root@79.108.160.103:/opt/family-manager/.env
# It is read by docker-compose.prod.yml and, through the caddy container's environment, by the
# Caddyfile. THIS FILE IS THE ONLY ONE THAT IS COMMITTED — the filled-in .env never is, and
# every value below is a placeholder that must be replaced. A deploy with these values as-is
# will come up with a guessable database password and no working object storage.
#
# The one secret that does NOT live here is the auth signing key: it is a compose secret read
# from ./secrets/auth-signing-key.pem so it never appears in `docker inspect`. Generate it with
#   openssl ecparam -name prime256v1 -genkey -noout -out secrets/auth-signing-key.pem
#   chmod 600 secrets/auth-signing-key.pem

# --- images ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The tag every service image is pinned to. deploy.sh overrides this from its argument (a full
# commit SHA) — a floating "latest" here is a fallback, not the intended state, because it makes
# "what is running" unanswerable and rollback impossible.
IMAGE_TAG=latest

# --- postgres ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Used both for the server's superuser and for every service's DATABASE_URL. Not published to
# the host, but "internal" is not a substitute for a password: every container on the compose
# network can reach it.
POSTGRES_USER=admin
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME_long_random_password
# The bootstrap database. The per-service databases (auth, family, finance, recipes) are created
# by postgres-init/ on first boot, not here.
POSTGRES_DB=postgres

# --- public hostnames --------------------------------------------------------
# One per service; Caddy issues a certificate for each. All four must have A records pointing at
# this box before Caddy can complete the ACME challenge.
AUTH_HOST=auth.nonamecat.pp.ua
FAMILY_HOST=family.nonamecat.pp.ua
FINANCE_HOST=finance.nonamecat.pp.ua
RECIPES_HOST=recipes.nonamecat.pp.ua

# Where Let's Encrypt sends certificate-expiry warnings. A dead address here means a silent
# renewal failure becomes an outage.
ACME_EMAIL=you@example.com

# --- object storage (Cloudflare R2, ADR 0007) --------------------------------
# Provisioned out of band: bucket, a public custom domain bound to it, and an API token scoped
# to Object Read & Write. The service never creates the bucket or sets a policy — R2 does not
# implement PutBucketPolicy and a scoped token cannot CreateBucket.
#
# Host only, no scheme, no bucket segment:
R2_ENDPOINT=CHANGE_ME_account_id.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=CHANGE_ME_r2_access_key_id
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=CHANGE_ME_r2_secret_access_key
R2_BUCKET=recipes
# The bucket's public custom domain, with scheme. A DIFFERENT host from R2_ENDPOINT: the S3
# endpoint serves no public reads, so pointing this at it produces image_url values that 401.
R2_PUBLIC_URL=https://images.nonamecat.pp.ua

# --- application ------------------------------------------------------------
# Currency family totals are reported in when accounts differ.
BASE_CURRENCY=EUR
# info, debug, warn, error. debug on this box is expensive: it is the log volume, not the CPU.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- sign-in lockout ---------------------------------------------------------
# Consecutive failed sign-ins for one address before the next attempt is refused, and the
# lockout that follows (doubling from base, capped at max). Defaults below are lower than the
# service's own, because this deployment serves one household: six wrong passwords in a row is
# not somebody mistyping.
#
# Per account, not per IP — everyone here shares one NAT, so an IP limit would lock the
# household out of its own accounts.
LOGIN_FAILURE_THRESHOLD=6
LOGIN_LOCKOUT_BASE=15s
LOGIN_LOCKOUT_MAX=10m

# JWKS is fetched in-network so token verification does not depend on DNS, Caddy or a valid
# certificate. Override only if auth is ever moved off this box.
# JWKS_URL=http://auth:8080/.well-known/jwks.json
